/* safety.lps — the supervisory tier of a Minecraft agent (Part III, §V.4).
 *
 * The bot's *reflexes* live in JavaScript: walking, jumping, swinging, path
 * following. They run at the game's tick rate and LPS never sees them. What
 * LPS owns is the part that has to be right rather than fast — the maintenance
 * goals and the constraints — and it owns them at its own pace, one cycle
 * every few hundred milliseconds.
 *
 * That split is §V.4's two tiers, and it is the same argument: the deliberative
 * layer can be wrong without being dangerous, because the commands it issues
 * are still filtered by constraints before anything acts on them.
 *
 * Events arrive from bot.mjs as observations; actions are read back and
 * executed by the bot. Both directions are the ordinary live-session API.
 */

fluents  health(_), food(_), light(_), holding(_), threat(_), animal(_),
	 position(_, _, _), mining(_), goal_block(_).
events   tick(_), food_level(_), spotted(_), lost(_), damaged(_), saw(_),
	 block_broken(_), inventory(_, _), night, day.
actions  flee(_), eat, attack(_), place_torch, say(_), stop_mining, resume_mining.

initially health(20), food(20), light(15), threat(none).

%  What the world tells us
damaged(H)        updates Old to H in health(Old).
tick(H)           updates Old to H in health(Old).
food_level(F)     updates Old to F in food(Old).
spotted(Mob)      updates Old to Mob in threat(Old).
saw(A)            initiates animal(A).
lost(_)           updates Old to none in threat(Old).
night             updates Old to 0 in light(Old).
day               updates Old to 15 in light(Old).

%  What we do about it
flee(_)           updates Old to none in threat(Old).
eat               updates Old to 20 in food(Old).
place_torch       updates Old to 14 in light(Old).

/* A maintenance goal that interrupts whatever the bot is doing.
 *
 * This is the demonstration §II.3 is about: the rule is not a suggestion the
 * planner may weigh against its current task. It fires the moment health drops,
 * and `stop_mining` goes out in the same cycle — the bot abandons its dig.
 */
if   health(H) at T, H < 8
then stop_mining from T to T2,
     flee(danger) from T2 to T3,
     say('breaking off — health low') from T3 to T4.

if   threat(Mob) at T, Mob \= none, health(H) at T, H < 15
then flee(Mob) from T to T2.

if   food(F) at T, F < 12
then eat from T to T2.

if   light(L) at T, L < 4
then place_torch from T to T2.

%  Hunger makes the bot hunt — which is where the constraint below earns its
%  keep, because a bot at four hearts should not be picking fights with cows.
if   food(F) at T, F < 12, animal(A) at T
then attack(A) from T to T2.

/* A constraint that blocks an action and can then explain itself.
 *
 * "Never attack a passive animal while you are hurt" — the bot has better
 * things to do at four hearts, and `why_not(happened(attack(cow)), T)` names
 * this clause rather than shrugging.
 */
false attack(Mob) from T1 to _, passive(Mob), health(H) at T1, H < 15.

%  …and never attack anything at all while fleeing: two actions that make no
%  sense together, which is an interference constraint rather than a
%  precondition on either one.
false attack(_), flee(_).

passive(cow).
passive(sheep).
passive(pig).
passive(chicken).
passive(villager).
